# Access Modifiers 🔒

TypeScript provides 3 access modifiers to control visibility:

| Modifier | Accessible Inside Class | Accessible in Subclasses | Accessible from Outside |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| `public` (Default) | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `protected` | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | ❌ No |
| `private` | ✅ Yes | ❌ No | ❌ No |

```typescript
class BankAccount {
  public owner: string;
  protected accountType: string;
  private balance: number;

  constructor(owner: string, balance: number) {
    this.owner = owner;
    this.accountType = "Standard Checking";
    this.balance = balance;
  }
}
```

# Protected vs Private in Inheritance 🧬

```typescript
class Parent {
  private secret: string = "Private to Parent";
  protected sharedSecret: string = "Accessible in Child";
}

class Child extends Parent {
  reveal() {
    // console.log(this.secret); // Compile Error: Property 'secret' is private!
    console.log("Child accessed:", this.sharedSecret); // Valid!
  }
}

const child = new Child();
child.reveal();
// console.log(child.sharedSecret); // Compile Error: Only accessible within class hierarchy!
```

