# Abstract Classes (`abstract`) 🏛️

An **abstract class** is a base class that **cannot be instantiated directly** using `new`. It serves as a blueprint for derived child classes.

```typescript
abstract class PaymentProcessor {
  constructor(public amount: number) {}

  // Concrete method (shared implementation)
  printReceipt() {
    console.log(`Receipt for: $${this.amount}`);
  }

  // Abstract method (must be implemented by subclasses!)
  abstract processPayment(): boolean;
}
```

Any class extending `PaymentProcessor` is forced to implement `processPayment()`!

# Implementing Abstract Subclasses 💳

```typescript
class CreditCardProcessor extends PaymentProcessor {
  constructor(amount: number, private cardNumber: string) {
    super(amount);
  }

  // Required implementation!
  processPayment(): boolean {
    console.log(`Charging $${this.amount} to card ending in ${this.cardNumber.slice(-4)}`);
    return true;
  }
}

const payment = new CreditCardProcessor(75, "4111222233334444");
payment.processPayment();
payment.printReceipt();
```

